            <ArticleTitle>Transient cortical blindness after intradiscal oxygen-ozone therapy.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>944-946</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.4103/0301-4738.198858</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A 54-year-old caucasian male developed bilateral blindness during an oxygen-ozone injection for disc herniation. The visual loss (VL) was immediately followed by severe frontal headache, vomiting, and nausea. The patient underestimated the VL showing Anton's syndrome, with a complete visual recovery after 2-month follow-up. Magnetic resonance data were consistent with recent ischemic lesions in bilateral vascular territories of posterior cerebral arteries.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
